      question: "Read the following passage, then answer: 'How does the writer use language and structure to create a sense of unease?'\n\n'The last house on the lane had always seemed to lean slightly, as though listening. Nobody went there. This was understood, though nobody had said it — not to children, not to strangers, not aloud. The garden had grown into itself years ago, consuming the gate, the path, the last ordinary things. Even birds chose routes around it, veering away at the last moment as though remembering something.' (12 marks)",
      answer: "Language: 'lean slightly, as though listening' — personification gives the house agency and intent, suggesting surveillance and threat. The simile creates unease because a house should not listen. 'Consuming the gate' — the verb 'consuming' makes the garden a predatory creature, devouring normal life. Structure: The short declarative 'Nobody went there' is abrupt and unexplained — the withholding of reason creates dread. 'Not to children, not to strangers, not aloud' — the anaphora of 'not' builds through three groups, culminating in 'not aloud', implying the topic is too dangerous to voice. The final image of birds avoiding it suggests something instinctive and primal about the fear. The paragraph builds from the house to the garden to the sky — an expanding circle of wrongness.",
